      "desc": "A velvet-pile prayer carpet with a vase and flowering-plant design in the central field. Five borders surround the floral motifs. The dominant colours are blue, red, green and white. It has five borders in all. It dates from the 19th century AD and originates from Persia. Persian rugs and carpets were woven side by side in a variety of types — by nomadic tribes, in village and town workshops, and in royal court manufactories. They therefore represent diverse and parallel traditions, and reflect the history of Iran, Persian culture and its various communities. Although the term “Persian carpet” is most often used for pile-woven textiles, it also covers flat-woven carpets and rugs such as kilim prints, Soumak work, and embroidered textiles like Suzani.",
